regions = [
 region("us", MW, 38.6, -91.5, 6.2,
 "Missouri's German wine country, the first AVA, and the rootstocks that saved Europe.",
 "Before Prohibition, the Midwest was a serious wine region. German immigrants in the Missouri River valley built Hermann into one of the largest wine towns in the country; Cincinnati's sparkling Catawba was famous on two continents; and Missouri and Texas scientists sent phylloxera-resistant American rootstocks that rescued French vineyards in the 1870s and 1880s. Today the region grows mostly hybrids and natives (Norton, Chambourcin, Vidal, Vignoles, Traminette and the University of Minnesota's cold-hardy Marquette, Frontenac and La Crescent) with vinifera on the Lake Michigan shore and Ohio's Lake Erie islands.\n\nAugusta, Missouri became the first American Viticultural Area on 20 June 1980, eight months before Napa Valley.",
 "German settlers founded Hermann in 1837; by the 1880s Stone Hill Winery was said to be the second-largest winery in the US. George Husmann, Hermann's horticulturist and later state entomologist Charles V. Riley's collaborator, promoted native grapes; Hermann Jaeger of Neosho bred phylloxera-resistant varieties and, with Husmann, shipped carloads of resistant cuttings to France, for which he was made a Chevalier of the Légion d'honneur in 1893. Nicholas Longworth planted Catawba at Cincinnati from 1825 and produced America's first celebrated sparkling wine; Longfellow wrote 'Catawba Wine' in his honour. By 1860 Ohio was the leading wine state. Black rot, the Civil War and Prohibition ended the era; Stone Hill grew mushrooms in its cellars.",
 "Continental climate with cold winters, hot humid summers and spring frosts. Loess bluffs along the Missouri; Ozark limestone and chert; glacial soils farther north.",
 "Norton (Cynthiana), Chambourcin, Vidal Blanc, Vignoles, Traminette, Catawba, Concord, Marquette, Frontenac, La Crescent, Seyval Blanc, Riesling.",
 "Norton reds, off-dry hybrid whites, port-style wines, ice wine in the north.",
 "Federal AVAs. Missouri's state grape is Norton.",
 ["Augusta", "Hermann", "Ozark Highlands", "Ozark Mountain", "Altus", "Arkansas Mountain", "Ohio River Valley", "Shawnee Hills", "Upper Mississippi River Valley", "Loess Hills District", "Indiana Uplands", "Kanawha River Valley", "Lake Wisconsin", "Wisconsin Ledge", "Alexandria Lakes", "Loramie Creek"], SRC, parent=None),
 region("us", GL, 44.0, -84.5, 6.2,
 "Lake-effect vineyards in Michigan and Ohio: Riesling on cherry-orchard peninsulas and island wineries in Lake Erie.",
 "The Great Lakes act as giant heat sinks: they delay spring budbreak, protect vines from the worst winter cold and extend autumn. Michigan has five AVAs, most famous for Riesling, Pinot Noir and sparkling wine on the Old Mission and Leelanau peninsulas near Traverse City. Ohio's Lake Erie islands (Isle St. George) and Grand River Valley grow vinifera and hybrids; the Lake Erie AVA spans three states.",
 "Lake Erie's Bass Islands had commercial vineyards by the 1850s; Golden Eagle Winery on Middle Bass Island (later Lonz) was billed as the largest in the US in the 1870s. In Michigan, Edward O'Keefe planted vinifera at Chateau Grand Traverse in 1974.",
 "Glacial sands and gravels; lake moderation within a few kilometres of the shore.",
 "Riesling, Pinot Noir, Chardonnay, Cabernet Franc, Pinot Gris, Gewürztraminer, Vidal, Catawba.",
 "Dry and off-dry Riesling, sparkling wine, ice wine, light reds.", "Federal AVAs.",
 ["Old Mission Peninsula", "Leelanau Peninsula", "Lake Michigan Shore", "Fennville", "Tip of the Mitt", "Lake Erie", "Isle St. George", "Grand River Valley"], SRC, parent=None),
 region("us", TX, 31.0, -99.5, 6.0,
 "Mission vines by 1650s El Paso, Munson's rootstocks, and a modern boom split between the High Plains and the Hill Country.",
 "Texas has well over 400 wineries and ranks among the top five US wine-producing states. The geography is split. About 80 to 85 percent of the grapes grow on the Texas High Plains around Lubbock, at 900 to 1,200 m, where hot days, cool nights, wind and sandy loam over caliche suit Tempranillo, Mourvèdre, Tannat, Viognier and Roussanne. Most wineries and tourists, though, are 600 km away in the Texas Hill Country west of Austin, around Fredericksburg, which crushes much of the High Plains fruit. Hail, spring frost and Pierce's disease are constant risks.\n\nTwo new Hill Country AVAs, Llano Uplift (2,096 square miles) and Hickory Sands District (193 square miles), were proposed in August 2026.",
 "Franciscan missionaries planted Mission grapes around El Paso del Norte in the 1650s or 1660s, more than a century before California. German immigrants in the Hill Country made wine in the 19th century, and Val Verde Winery at Del Rio (1883) is the state's oldest continuously operating winery. T.V. Munson of Denison bred hundreds of grape varieties and identified Texas native species whose rootstocks helped save French vineyards from phylloxera; France made him a Chevalier du Mérite Agricole in 1888, and Cognac became Denison's sister city. Prohibition ended the industry; Llano Estacado (1976) and Pheasant Ridge near Lubbock started the revival.",
 "High Plains: caliche, sandy loam, high elevation, wide diurnal swings. Hill Country: limestone, granite (Llano Uplift) and sandy soils, hotter and more humid.",
 "Tempranillo, Cabernet Sauvignon, Mourvèdre, Tannat, Merlot, Sangiovese, Viognier, Roussanne, Blanc du Bois, Black Spanish (Lenoir).",
 "Mediterranean-style reds and whites; rosé.", "Federal AVAs; 'Texas' on a label requires 75 percent Texas fruit, and there is pressure from producers to raise it.",
 ["Texas High Plains", "Texas Hill Country", "Fredericksburg in the Texas Hill Country", "Bell Mountain", "Texas Davis Mountains", "Escondido Valley", "Texoma", "Mesilla Valley"], SRC, parent=None),
 region("us", SW, 34.0, -108.5, 5.6,
 "High-desert vineyards in Arizona, New Mexico and Colorado, and the oldest wine tradition in the United States.",
 "The Southwest grows grapes at altitude to escape the heat: Arizona's Sonoita, Willcox and Verde Valley AVAs sit at 1,000 to 1,500 m, Colorado's Grand Valley and West Elks at 1,300 to 2,000 m, and New Mexico's vineyards along the Rio Grande at 1,100 to 1,700 m. Rhône, Spanish and Italian varieties thrive; frost, hail and monsoon rains are the main hazards.",
 "Franciscan friars planted the first vines in what is now the United States in New Mexico around 1629, at the Piro pueblo mission of Senecú south of Socorro, to make sacramental wine; New Mexico wine thus predates California by more than a century. Floods and Prohibition ended its 19th-century industry. Arizona's modern wine began with Dr. Gordon Dutt's experimental vineyard at Sonoita in the 1970s; Sonoita became Arizona's first AVA in 1984. Colorado's Palisade growers planted vinifera in the 1890s, before Prohibition, and again from the 1970s.",
 "Volcanic, granitic and alluvial soils; intense sun, low humidity, big day-night swings; late spring frosts.",
 "Syrah, Grenache, Mourvèdre, Tempranillo, Malbec, Cabernet Franc, Riesling, Malvasia Bianca, Chardonnay; Mission in New Mexico.",
 "Rhône and Spanish-style reds; New Mexico sparkling wine.", "Federal AVAs.",
 ["Sonoita", "Willcox", "Verde Valley", "Middle Rio Grande Valley", "Mesilla Valley", "Mimbres Valley", "Grand Valley", "West Elks"], SRC, parent=None)]

A = []
A.append(ava("Augusta", MW, "The first American Viticultural Area, approved on 20 June 1980, eight months before Napa Valley. It covers about 15,000 acres around the town of Augusta on the Missouri River west of St. Louis, founded by German settlers in the 1830s; before Prohibition it had a dozen wineries. Loess-capped bluffs and a valley moderated by the river grow Norton, Chambourcin, Vignoles and Chardonel. Mount Pleasant Estates (1859) is the anchor winery.", sub="Missouri", styles=["red", "white", "fortified"], dom="red", grapes="Norton, Chambourcin, Vignoles, Chardonel, Vidal", producers=["Mount Pleasant Estates", "Augusta Winery", "Balducci", "Noboleis"], nerd="Augusta won the race to be first partly because the 1978 rules were brand new and its petition was simple: a small, well-defined valley with a clear historical name."))
A.append(ava("Hermann", MW, "An AVA (1983) of about 51,000 acres (80 square miles) along the Missouri River around the German-founded town of Hermann (1837). By the 1880s Stone Hill Winery was one of the largest in the country, winning medals at world fairs, and the town's Norton, known locally as Cynthiana, became famous; Missouri adopted Norton as its official state grape. Stone Hill and Hermannhof restored the 19th-century cellars after Prohibition.", sub="Missouri", styles=["red", "white", "fortified"], dom="red", grapes="Norton, Chambourcin, Vignoles, Catawba, Vidal", producers=["Stone Hill", "Hermannhof", "Adam Puchta", "Dierberg Star Lane (Hermann)"], nerd="Adam Puchta Winery (1855) claims to be the oldest continuously family-owned winery in the US."))
A.append(ava("Ozark Highlands", MW, "A large AVA (1987) of about 1.85 million acres on the Ozark Plateau of south-central Missouri, around St. James, with chert and limestone soils. Italian immigrants at Rosati and St. James grew Concord and Catawba; St. James Winery is one of Missouri's largest.", sub="Missouri", styles=["red", "white", "sweet"], dom="white", grapes="Norton, Catawba, Concord, Vignoles, Chambourcin", producers=["St. James Winery"]))
A.append(ava("Ozark Mountain", MW, "By area the largest AVA in the United States: about 33 million acres (around 52,000 square miles) of the Ozark Plateau in Missouri, Arkansas and Oklahoma, approved in 1986. It contains the Hermann, Ozark Highlands, Altus and Arkansas Mountain AVAs and is used mostly as a catch-all for wines from the Ozarks.", sub="Missouri, Arkansas, Oklahoma", styles=["red", "white"], dom="red", grapes="Norton, Chambourcin, Vignoles, Catawba", contains=["Hermann", "Ozark Highlands", "Arkansas Mountain", "Altus"]))
A.append(ava("Arkansas Mountain", MW, "An AVA (1986) of about 2.9 million acres in the Arkansas River valley and the Boston Mountains of northwestern Arkansas, petitioned by Al Wiederkehr. Swiss and German immigrants planted here in the 1880s.", sub="Arkansas", styles=["white", "red"], dom="white", grapes="Cynthiana, Vignoles, Chardonnay, Riesling, Muscadine", contains=["Altus"]))
A.append(ava("Altus", MW, "A small AVA (1984) around the town of Altus in western Arkansas, settled by Swiss and German immigrants in the 1880s. Wiederkehr Wine Cellars (1880) and Post Winery (1880) are among the oldest in the South; Mount Bethel and Chateau aux Arc also operate here.", sub="Arkansas", styles=["white", "red", "sweet"], dom="white", grapes="Muscadine, Cynthiana, Niagara, Chardonnay, Zinfandel", producers=["Wiederkehr", "Post Familie", "Chateau aux Arc"]))
A.append(ava("Ohio River Valley", MW, "A huge AVA (1983) of about 16 million acres along the Ohio River in Indiana, Kentucky, Ohio and West Virginia. This was the scene of America's first commercial wine boom: Swiss settlers at Vevay, Indiana planted vines in the early 1800s, and Nicholas Longworth's Catawba at Cincinnati made 'sparkling Catawba' famous in the 1840s and 1850s. Today it is a broad umbrella for scattered wineries.", sub="Indiana, Kentucky, Ohio, West Virginia", styles=["red", "white"], dom="red", grapes="Chambourcin, Vidal, Catawba, Traminette, Cabernet Franc", contains=["Kanawha River Valley"]))
A.append(ava("Kanawha River Valley", MW, "An AVA (1986) in the Kanawha River valley of West Virginia, around Charleston, within the Ohio River Valley AVA. A small number of wineries grow hybrids.", sub="West Virginia", styles=["white", "red"], dom="white", grapes="Seyval Blanc, Chambourcin, Vidal"))
A.append(ava("Indiana Uplands", MW, "An AVA (2013) of about 4,600 square miles in the hills of southern Indiana, carved out of the Ohio River Valley AVA. At approval it had 19 vineyards (about 200 acres) and 17 wineries, including Oliver Winery, the state's largest, and Huber's Orchard. Limestone karst geology; Traminette, Chambourcin and Vidal.", sub="Indiana", styles=["white", "red", "sweet"], dom="white", grapes="Traminette, Chambourcin, Vidal, Cabernet Franc", producers=["Oliver Winery", "Huber's", "Turtle Run"]))
A.append(ava("Shawnee Hills", MW, "An AVA (2006) in the unglaciated hills of southern Illinois, between the Ohio and Mississippi rivers. The region had about 1,250 acres of vineyards in 1890; today about a dozen wineries grow Chambourcin, Norton and Traminette.", sub="Illinois", styles=["red", "white"], dom="red", grapes="Chambourcin, Norton, Traminette, Vidal"))
A.append(ava("Upper Mississippi River Valley", MW, "The second-largest AVA by area (2009), about 19 million acres along the Mississippi in Illinois, Iowa, Minnesota and Wisconsin, including the unglaciated 'Driftless Area'. Cold winters limit growers to hybrids bred at the University of Minnesota and by Elmer Swenson, such as Marquette, Frontenac, La Crescent and Brianna.", sub="Illinois, Iowa, Minnesota, Wisconsin", styles=["red", "white"], dom="red", grapes="Marquette, Frontenac, La Crescent, St. Croix, Edelweiss", contains=["Lake Wisconsin"]))
A.append(ava("Lake Wisconsin", MW, "A small AVA (1994) of about 32,000 acres in Columbia and Dane counties, Wisconsin, on the Wisconsin River near Prairie du Sac. Wollersheim Winery occupies the site where the Hungarian Agoston Haraszthy planted vines in the 1840s before he left for California; its hillside caves were dug in 1858.", sub="Wisconsin", styles=["red", "white"], dom="red", grapes="Marquette, Maréchal Foch, St. Pepin, La Crescent", producers=["Wollersheim"]))
A.append(ava("Wisconsin Ledge", MW, "An AVA (2012) of about 3,800 square miles (2.4 million acres) in northeastern Wisconsin along the Niagara Escarpment, including the Door Peninsula. Lake Michigan and Green Bay moderate the climate; cold-hardy hybrids dominate.", sub="Wisconsin", styles=["white", "red"], dom="white", grapes="Marquette, La Crescent, St. Pepin, Frontenac"))
A.append(ava("Alexandria Lakes", MW, "A small AVA (2005) of about 9,000 acres among the glacial lakes near Alexandria, Minnesota, created around Carlos Creek Winery and among the most northerly AVAs in the US. Cold-hardy hybrids.", sub="Minnesota", styles=["red", "white"], dom="red", grapes="Frontenac, La Crescent, Marquette"))
A.append(ava("Loess Hills District", MW, "An AVA (2016) of about 4.6 million acres (some 7,200 square miles) along the Missouri River in western Iowa and northwestern Missouri, defined by its extraordinarily deep wind-blown loess soils, which form steep bluffs. At approval it had 66 vineyards on 112 acres and 13 wineries.", sub="Iowa and Missouri", styles=["red", "white"], dom="red", grapes="Marquette, La Crescent, Frontenac, Edelweiss"))
A.append(ava("Loramie Creek", MW, "A tiny AVA (1982) in Shelby County, western Ohio, created for a single farm winery; no longer commercially active, but still on the TTB list.", sub="Ohio", styles=["white"], dom="white", grapes="Seyval Blanc"))
A.append(ava("Old Mission Peninsula", GL, "A narrow, 30 km peninsula (1987) jutting into Grand Traverse Bay north of Traverse City. Lake Michigan surrounds it on three sides, delaying spring and extending autumn; cherry orchards share the slopes with Riesling, Pinot Gris, Pinot Noir and Chardonnay. Edward O'Keefe planted vinifera at Chateau Grand Traverse in 1974, Michigan's first commercial vinifera vineyard.", sub="Michigan", styles=["white", "red", "sparkling", "sweet"], dom="white", grapes="Riesling, Pinot Gris, Chardonnay, Pinot Noir, Gewürztraminer", producers=["Chateau Grand Traverse", "Brys Estate", "Mari Vineyards", "Bowers Harbor", "2 Lads"]))
A.append(ava("Leelanau Peninsula", GL, "A peninsula AVA (1982) between Lake Michigan and Grand Traverse Bay, home to dozens of wineries. Larry Mawby, who petitioned for the AVA, is known for sparkling wine; Riesling, Pinot Noir and Gewürztraminer are the main grapes.", sub="Michigan", styles=["white", "sparkling", "red"], dom="white", grapes="Riesling, Pinot Noir, Chardonnay, Pinot Blanc, Gewürztraminer", producers=["L. Mawby", "Black Star Farms", "Blustone", "Shady Lane"]))
A.append(ava("Lake Michigan Shore", GL, "An AVA (1983) in southwestern Michigan near Lake Michigan, where lake winds protect vines from cold. It is Michigan's most productive area, with St. Julian (1921) and Tabor Hill (1968) among its wineries.", sub="Michigan", styles=["white", "red", "sweet"], dom="white", grapes="Riesling, Chardonnay, Cabernet Franc, Vidal, Concord", producers=["St. Julian", "Tabor Hill", "Domaine Berrien"], contains=["Fennville"]))
A.append(ava("Fennville", GL, "One of the first AVAs (1981), a small area near Lake Michigan in Allegan County around Fenn Valley Vineyards, which petitioned for it. Sandy soils and lake moderation.", sub="Lake Michigan Shore", styles=["white", "red"], dom="white", grapes="Riesling, Chardonnay, Pinot Gris, Chambourcin", producers=["Fenn Valley"]))
A.append(ava("Tip of the Mitt", GL, "A cold AVA (2016) of about 2,760 square miles (1.75 million acres) at the northern tip of Michigan's Lower Peninsula, between Lakes Michigan and Huron. Cold-hardy hybrids dominate; 41 vineyards (94 acres) and 8 wineries at approval.", sub="Michigan", styles=["white", "red"], dom="white", grapes="Marquette, Frontenac, La Crescent"))
A.append(ava("Isle St. George", GL, "An island AVA (1982) of about 700 acres covering North Bass Island in Lake Erie, close to the Canadian border. The lake keeps winters mild and autumns long, and vines have grown here since the 1850s. Meier's Wine Cellars, which petitioned for the AVA, farmed much of the island's land in Catawba, Riesling and Chardonnay.", sub="Ohio", styles=["white", "red"], dom="white", grapes="Catawba, Riesling, Chardonnay, Pinot Gris"))
A.append(ava("Grand River Valley", GL, "An AVA (1983) in northeast Ohio along the Grand River near Lake Erie, where lake moderation and gravelly soils allow vinifera. Debonné Vineyards and Ferrante are leading producers of Riesling and ice wine.", sub="Ohio", styles=["white", "red", "sweet"], dom="white", grapes="Riesling, Chardonnay, Pinot Gris, Cabernet Franc, Vidal", producers=["Debonné", "Ferrante", "Laurello", "M Cellars"]))
A.append(ava("Texas High Plains", TX, "The engine of Texas wine (1993), about 8.9 million acres on the Llano Estacado around Lubbock, at 900 to 1,200 m. It grows the large majority of Texas wine grapes. Hot days, cool nights, wind and low humidity keep disease down, though hail and spring freeze are serious risks; soils are sandy loam over caliche. Tempranillo, Mourvèdre, Tannat, Viognier and Roussanne lead.", sub="Texas", styles=["red", "white", "rose"], dom="red", grapes="Tempranillo, Mourvèdre, Tannat, Cabernet Sauvignon, Viognier, Roussanne, Sangiovese", producers=["Llano Estacado", "McPherson", "Pheasant Ridge", "William Chris (sourcing)", "Lost Draw"]))
A.append(ava("Texas Hill Country", TX, "By area the largest AVA wholly within one state (1991), about 9.3 million acres (some 14,500 square miles) west of Austin and San Antonio, centred on Fredericksburg. It is the heart of Texas wine tourism, with more than 100 wineries, though only a small share of the state's vineyards; many wineries use High Plains grapes. Limestone and granite soils; hot, humid summers. Two new AVAs, Llano Uplift and Hickory Sands District, were proposed inside it in 2026.", sub="Texas", styles=["red", "white", "rose"], dom="red", grapes="Tempranillo, Mourvèdre, Sangiovese, Viognier, Blanc du Bois, Cabernet Sauvignon", producers=["Becker", "Fall Creek", "Pedernales", "Duchman", "William Chris", "Kuhlken", "Ron Yates"], contains=["Fredericksburg in the Texas Hill Country", "Bell Mountain"]))
A.append(ava("Fredericksburg in the Texas Hill Country", TX, "A small AVA (1988) of about 66,000 acres (roughly 100 square miles) around the German-founded town of Fredericksburg (1846). Sandy, granite-derived soils and the town's tourist trade have made it the epicentre of Hill Country wine; Pedernales Cellars petitioned for it.", sub="Texas Hill Country", styles=["red", "white"], dom="red", grapes="Tempranillo, Cabernet Sauvignon, Viognier, Mourvèdre", producers=["Pedernales", "Grape Creek", "Becker (nearby)"]))
A.append(ava("Bell Mountain", TX, "Texas's first AVA (1986), a single granite-and-sand hill north of Fredericksburg owned by the Oberhelman family. It would be enclosed by the proposed Llano Uplift AVA.", sub="Texas Hill Country", styles=["red", "white"], dom="red", grapes="Cabernet Sauvignon, Chardonnay"))
A.append(ava("Texas Davis Mountains", TX, "A high, remote AVA (1998) in the Davis Mountains of West Texas, around Fort Davis, with vineyards at about 1,500 m on volcanic soils, among the highest in the state.", sub="Texas", styles=["red"], dom="red", grapes="Cabernet Sauvignon, Merlot, Syrah"))
A.append(ava("Escondido Valley", TX, "A small AVA (1992) in Pecos County, West Texas, where large vineyards were planted on University of Texas land near Fort Stockton in the 1980s, at about 900 m. The desert setting means intense heat and irrigation; the vineyards have long supplied grapes to Texas wineries.", sub="Texas", styles=["red", "white"], dom="red", grapes="Cabernet Sauvignon, Chenin Blanc"))
A.append(ava("Texoma", TX, "An AVA (2005) of about 3,350 square miles along the Red River on the Texas-Oklahoma border, around Lake Texoma and Denison, where T.V. Munson worked. Pierce's disease limits vinifera; Munson's hybrids and Blanc du Bois do better.", sub="Texas", styles=["red", "white"], dom="red", grapes="Blanc du Bois, Black Spanish, Cabernet Sauvignon, Munson hybrids"))
A.append(ava("Mesilla Valley", SW, "An AVA (1985) along the Rio Grande from Las Cruces, New Mexico into far West Texas near El Paso, where Spanish friars planted Mission vines in the 17th century. Hot desert summers; St. Clair and La Viña are among its wineries.", sub="New Mexico and Texas", styles=["red", "white"], dom="red", grapes="Mission, Cabernet Sauvignon, Zinfandel, Muscat"))
A.append(ava("Middle Rio Grande Valley", SW, "An AVA (1988) along the Rio Grande from Santa Fe's southern edge through Albuquerque to Socorro. Spanish friars planted Mission vines near Socorro around 1629, the first in what became the United States. Gruet, founded by a Champagne family from Bethon in 1984, makes traditional-method sparkling wine here.", sub="New Mexico", styles=["sparkling", "red", "white"], dom="sparkling", grapes="Chardonnay, Pinot Noir, Mission, Cabernet Sauvignon", producers=["Gruet", "Casa Rondeña", "Milagro"]))
A.append(ava("Mimbres Valley", SW, "An AVA (1985) in the Mimbres River valley of southwestern New Mexico around Deming. Large vineyards here, many owned by European investors in the 1980s, supply much of New Mexico's wine grapes.", sub="New Mexico", styles=["red", "white"], dom="red", grapes="Cabernet Sauvignon, Zinfandel, Chardonnay", producers=["St. Clair"]))
A.append(ava("Sonoita", SW, "Arizona's first AVA (1984), a grassland basin southeast of Tucson at about 1,500 m. Dr. Gordon Dutt of the University of Arizona planted test vineyards in the 1970s and founded Sonoita Vineyards in 1983. Red, iron-rich soils and monsoon rains in July and August; Callaghan Vineyards and Dos Cabezas made it known.", sub="Arizona", styles=["red", "white"], dom="red", grapes="Syrah, Grenache, Tempranillo, Malvasia Bianca, Cabernet Sauvignon", producers=["Sonoita Vineyards", "Callaghan", "Dos Cabezas", "Rune"]))
A.append(ava("Willcox", SW, "Arizona's main growing area (2016), about 526,000 acres in a high basin around a dry lake bed (Willcox Playa) at about 1,300 to 1,600 m. At approval it had 21 vineyards on about 454 acres and 18 wineries; it now grows the majority of Arizona's wine grapes, mostly Rhône and Spanish varieties.", sub="Arizona", styles=["red", "white"], dom="red", grapes="Syrah, Grenache, Mourvèdre, Tempranillo, Malvasia Bianca, Viognier", producers=["Bodega Pierce", "Sand-Reckoner", "Carlson Creek", "Aridus"]))
A.append(ava("Verde Valley", SW, "A valley AVA (2021) of about 200 square miles along the Verde River in Yavapai County, near Cottonwood and Jerome, at roughly 1,000 to 1,500 m. Maynard James Keenan of Caduceus Cellars helped lead its rise. At approval it had 24 vineyards (about 125 acres) and 11 wineries.", sub="Arizona", styles=["red", "white"], dom="red", grapes="Syrah, Grenache, Malvasia Bianca, Tempranillo", producers=["Caduceus", "Page Springs", "Alcantara"]))
A.append(ava("Grand Valley", SW, "Colorado's main wine area (1991), along the Colorado River around Palisade and Grand Junction at about 1,400 m. Cold air drains down the river canyon at night and warm air off the Book Cliffs reduces frost, making it fruit country (Palisade peaches) as well as wine country. Riesling, Cabernet Franc, Syrah and Merlot.", sub="Colorado", styles=["red", "white"], dom="red", grapes="Riesling, Cabernet Franc, Syrah, Merlot, Cabernet Sauvignon", producers=["Colterris", "Carlson", "Plum Creek", "Colorado Cellars", "Sauvage Spectrum"]))
A.append(ava("West Elks", SW, "One of the highest AVAs in the US (2001), along the North Fork of the Gunnison River around Paonia and Hotchkiss, at about 1,700 to 1,900 m. Cool and high, it grows Pinot Noir, Riesling and Gewürztraminer; Stone Cottage and Azura are among its small wineries.", sub="Colorado", styles=["white", "red"], dom="white", grapes="Pinot Noir, Riesling, Gewürztraminer, Chardonnay", producers=["Stone Cottage", "Black Bridge", "Terror Creek"]))
A.append(ava("Ulupalakua", "Hawaii", "The smallest AVA in the United States (2021), about 70 acres on the leeward slopes of Haleakalā on Maui, at around 600 m, with about 16 acres of vines at approval. It is essentially MauiWine's estate on the historic Ulupalakua Ranch, where Emil Tedeschi planted grapes in 1974; volcanic soils and trade winds suit Syrah, Malbec and Chenin Blanc, and MauiWine also makes pineapple wine.", sub="Hawaii", styles=["red", "white", "sparkling"], dom="red", grapes="Syrah, Malbec, Grenache, Chenin Blanc, Viognier", producers=["MauiWine"]))

S = []
S.append(site("stone-hill-winery", "us", "Stone Hill Winery", "historic", "Hermann", 38.6966, -91.4462,
 "Founded in 1847 by German immigrant Michael Poeschel above Hermann; by the 1880s it was billed as the second-largest winery in the US, with wines winning at world fairs. During Prohibition its arched cellars were used to grow mushrooms; the Held family revived it in 1965.", cls="Founded 1847", producers=["Stone Hill"]))
S.append(site("mount-pleasant", "us", "Mount Pleasant Estates", "historic", "Augusta", 38.5705, -90.8856,
 "Founded in 1859 by Friedrich Münch, a German pastor and writer, in Augusta, the town that became America's first AVA in 1980. Its 19th-century cellars survive.", cls="Founded 1859", producers=["Mount Pleasant"]))
S.append(site("jaeger-neosho", "us", "Hermann Jaeger's Neosho vineyards", "historic", "Ozark Mountain", 36.8665, -94.3702,
 "Swiss-born Hermann Jaeger settled near Neosho in 1865 and bred more than 100 grape varieties from native species. Working with George Husmann, he shipped about 17 boxcars of phylloxera-resistant cuttings to France, and in 1893 France made him a Chevalier of the Légion d'honneur. He disappeared in 1895.", cls="Phylloxera rescue, 1870s to 1880s", q="approx"))
S.append(site("munson-vineyard", "us", "T.V. Munson Memorial Vineyard", "historic", "Texoma", 33.7084, -96.6586,
 "At Denison, Thomas Volney Munson studied native Texas grapes and bred hundreds of varieties. His rootstock recommendations helped France replant after phylloxera, earning him the Mérite Agricole in 1888; Cognac is Denison's sister city. Grayson College maintains this vineyard of his varieties.", cls="Phylloxera rescue", producers=[]))
S.append(site("val-verde-winery", "us", "Val Verde Winery", "historic", "Texas (no AVA)", 29.3461, -100.8958,
 "Frank Qualia, an Italian immigrant, founded Val Verde at Del Rio in 1883; it is the oldest continuously operating winery in Texas and survived Prohibition selling table grapes.", cls="Founded 1883"))
S.append(site("llano-estacado", "us", "Llano Estacado Winery", "estate", "Texas High Plains", 33.4755, -101.7926,
 "Founded near Lubbock in 1976 by Texas Tech professors and others, it led the modern Texas wine revival and is one of the state's largest wineries.", cls="Founded 1976", producers=["Llano Estacado"]))
S.append(site("eden-park-longworth", "us", "Eden Park (Longworth's Garden of Eden vineyard)", "historic", "Ohio River Valley", 39.1152, -84.4932,
 "Nicholas Longworth's hillside vineyard, which he called the Garden of Eden, grew the Catawba for his sparkling wine, America's first famous wine in the 1840s and 1850s. The land became Cincinnati's Eden Park.", cls="Catawba, 1820s to 1860s", q="approx"))
S.append(site("lonz-winery", "us", "Lonz Winery (Golden Eagle), Middle Bass Island", "historic", "Lake Erie", 41.6713, -82.8105,
 "Golden Eagle Winery on Middle Bass Island was billed as America's largest winery in the 1870s; the castle-like Lonz building of 1942 stands over its old cellars and is now a state park.", cls="Founded 1860s"))
S.append(site("chateau-grand-traverse", "us", "Chateau Grand Traverse", "estate", "Old Mission Peninsula", 44.8649, -85.5200,
 "Edward O'Keefe planted Michigan's first commercial vinifera vineyard here on the Old Mission Peninsula in 1974, when most local growers relied on hybrids and cherries. Riesling, Chardonnay and Pinot Noir on the lake-moderated slopes proved that northern Michigan could make fine wine, and the family still runs the estate.", cls="Founded 1974", producers=["Chateau Grand Traverse"]))
S.append(site("wollersheim", "us", "Wollersheim Winery (Haraszthy site)", "historic", "Lake Wisconsin", 43.2891, -89.7073,
 "Agoston Haraszthy planted vines on this hillside above the Wisconsin River in the 1840s before he left for California; Peter Kehl dug the stone cellars in 1858. The Wollersheim family has run it since 1972.", q="approx", producers=["Wollersheim"]))
S.append(site("senecu", "us", "Senecú mission area (first vines in the US, c. 1629)", "historic", "New Mexico (no AVA)", 33.8000, -106.8800,
 "Around 1629 Franciscan friars García de San Francisco and Antonio de Arteaga planted Mission grapes at the Piro pueblo mission of San Antonio de Senecú south of Socorro, to make sacramental wine: the first wine grapes in what became the United States. The exact site is lost.", cls="c. 1629", q="approx"))
S.append(site("gruet", "us", "Gruet Winery", "house", "Middle Rio Grande Valley", 35.1791, -106.5814,
 "The Gruet family, Champagne growers from Bethon, planted vineyards near Truth or Consequences in southern New Mexico in 1984 at about 1,300 m and makes traditional-method sparkling wine in Albuquerque. Gruet Brut and Blanc de Noirs became some of the best-value American sparklers.", producers=["Gruet"]))
S.append(site("sonoita-vineyards", "us", "Sonoita Vineyards", "estate", "Sonoita", 31.6234, -110.5483,
 "Dr. Gordon Dutt, a University of Arizona soil scientist, ran test plots that led to Arizona's first AVA (1984) and founded this winery at Elgin in 1983.", cls="Founded 1983", producers=["Sonoita Vineyards"]))
S.append(site("mauiwine", "us", "MauiWine (Ulupalakua Ranch)", "estate", "Ulupalakua", 20.6482, -156.3977,
 "Emil Tedeschi and rancher Pardee Erdman founded Tedeschi Vineyards on the slopes of Haleakalā in 1974; the tasting room is the 19th-century King's Cottage, built for King Kalākaua's visits, a short drive below the estate vineyards that make up America's smallest AVA.", producers=["MauiWine"]))
S.append(site("palisade", "us", "Palisade wine country", "landscape", "Grand Valley", 39.1103, -108.3509,
 "Orchards and vineyards on the Colorado River below the Book Cliffs, where warm air and canyon breezes protect fruit from frost. Most of Colorado's wine grapes grow within a few kilometres of this town.", q="approx"))
